<body>

    <h1>Fish and Chips</h1>

    <img src="https://www.daringgourmet.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/Fish-and-Chips-1.jpg">

    <section>
        <details>
            <summary>Fish</summary>

            <img src="https://www.daringgourmet.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/Fish-and-Chips-2.jpg"
                 alt="Beer-battered fish">

            <h2>Ingredients</h2>
            <p>1 cup all-purpose flour</p>
            <p>1 teaspoon baking powder</p>
            <p>1 teaspoon salt</p>
            <p>1 1/4 cups light beer, very cold</p>
            <p>Extra flour for dredging, chilled</p>
            <p>1 1/2 pounds fresh cod, haddock, or halibut, cut into 4 pieces and patted dry</p>
            <p>Oil for frying</p>

            <h2>Instructions</h2>
            <p>Combine the flour, baking powder, and salt in a large flat bowl. Pour in the cold beer and whisk until smooth. Use the batter immediately.</p>
            <p>In another large flat bowl, add some extra flour for dredging.</p>
            <p>Blot the fish with paper towels to remove excess moisture. Thoroughly coat all sides of the fish in flour and shake off the excess.</p>
            <p>Dip the fish into the beer batter to coat all sides, allowing some excess batter to drip off.</p>
            <p>Heat oil to between 350°F and 375°F. Carefully lower the fish into the oil, waiting a few seconds before releasing it.</p>
            <p>Fry for 5–8 minutes in a deep fryer, or about 2 minutes on each side in a frying pan, until golden.</p>
            <p>Remove the fish with a slotted spoon. Let the oil drain, place the fish on paper towels briefly, and serve immediately.</p>
        </details>
    </section>

    <section>
        <details>
            <summary>Chips</summary>

            <img src="https://www.daringgourmet.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/Fish-and-Chips-3.jpg"
                 alt="Golden chips">

            <h2>Ingredients</h2>
            <p>4 large Russet potatoes, or another high-starch, low-moisture potato</p>
            <p>Salt for sprinkling</p>
            <p>Oil for frying</p>

            <h2>Instructions</h2>
            <p>Peel the potatoes and slice them thickly. Place them in a large bowl of cold water until ready to fry.</p>
            <p>Heat the oven to 200°F. Heat the oil in a deep fryer or a large heavy pan until it reaches 325°F.</p>
            <p>Drain the potatoes thoroughly and blot them with paper towels to remove excess water.</p>
            <p>Fry the potatoes in small batches for 2–3 minutes until pale and softened.</p>
            <p>Remove the potatoes with a slotted spoon and let them cool to room temperature.</p>
            <p>Increase the oil temperature to 375°F. Fry the potatoes again in small batches for another 2–3 minutes, until golden.</p>
            <p>Remove the chips, sprinkle with salt while hot, and keep them in the warmed oven while frying the fish.</p>
        </details>
    </section>

    <section>
        <details>
            <summary>Put together &amp; enjoy</summary>

            <img src="https://www.daringgourmet.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/Fish-and-Chips-1.jpg"
                 alt="Fish and chips served together">

            <h2>To Serve</h2>
            <p>Serve the fried fish with the chips.</p>
            <p>Sprinkle quality British malt vinegar over the fish and chips.</p>
            <p>If desired, serve with British mushy peas or tartar sauce.</p>
            <p>Serve immediately while the fish is hot and crispy.</p>
        </details>
    </section>

